<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William, Duke of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1675–1713</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William was Duke of Mecklenburg-Schwerin from 1692 to 1713. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg">House of Mecklenburg</a> died out in 1695, he disputed its inheritance with his uncle Adolf Frederick. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> of 1701 settled the quarrel: Frederick William kept the larger western share around Schwerin and Rostock, while Adolf Frederick received Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. The division lasted until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-duke-of-mecklenburg-schwerin.jpg" alt="Frederick William, Duke of Mecklenburg-Schwerin" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Wilhelm_I._von_Mecklenburg-Schwerin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
